   a plurality of steel wires twisted together to form a multi-strand cable body,  
   wherein at least one of the plurality of steel wires is formed from an Fe-based steel alloy comprising, in percent by mass:  
   carbon (C) in an amount of [C range];  
   silicon (Si) in an amount of [Si range];  
   manganese (Mn) in an amount of [Mn range];  
   chromium (Cr) in an amount of [Cr range];  
   sulfur (S) in an amount not greater than [S limit];  
   phosphorus (P) in an amount not greater than [P limit]; and  
   balance iron and unavoidable impurities,  
   wherein the at least one steel wire has a metallurgical structure comprising retained austenite, tempered martensite, and untempered martensite.

2. **The cable bolt of claim 1**, wherein each of the plurality of steel wires is formed from the Fe-based steel alloy.

3. **The cable bolt of claim 1**, wherein the retained austenite is present in an amount effective to improve ductility and energy absorption of the cable bolt under deformation of the rock mass.

4. **The cable bolt of claim 1**, wherein the tempered martensite and untempered martensite are present in relative amounts effective to provide both high tensile strength and controlled elongation of the cable bolt.

5. **The cable bolt of claim 1**, wherein the plurality of steel wires are helically twisted around a longitudinal axis of the cable body.

6. **The cable bolt of claim 1**, wherein the cable body comprises at least three twisted steel wires.

7. **The cable bolt of claim 1**, wherein the cable body comprises seven twisted steel wires.

8. **The cable bolt of claim 1**, wherein the at least one steel wire has a tensile strength sufficient for use as a rock reinforcement cable bolt in underground mining, tunneling, or civil excavation.

9. **The cable bolt of claim 1**, wherein the Fe-based steel alloy contains sulfur and phosphorus only as impurity elements.

10. **The cable bolt of claim 1**, wherein the at least one steel wire is heat treated to form the structure comprising retained austenite, tempered martensite, and untempered martensite.

11. **The cable bolt of claim 10**, wherein the heat treatment comprises quenching and tempering under conditions selected to retain austenite while forming both tempered and untempered martensite.

12. **The cable bolt of claim 1**, wherein the cable bolt is configured to be installed in a borehole in the rock mass with grout, resin, or mechanical anchoring.

13. **A rock support system**, comprising:  
   a borehole formed in a rock mass;  
   the cable bolt of claim 1 positioned in the borehole; and  
   an anchoring material disposed between the cable bolt and the rock mass.

14. **A steel wire for a multi-strand cable bolt**, the steel wire being formed from an Fe-based steel alloy comprising, in percent by mass:  
   C: [C range];  
   Si: [Si range];  
   Mn: [Mn range];  
   Cr: [Cr range];  
   S: not greater than [S limit];  
   P: not greater than [P limit];  
   balance Fe and unavoidable impurities,  
   wherein the steel wire has a microstructure comprising retained austenite, tempered martensite, and untempered martensite.

15. **A method of manufacturing a cable bolt for supporting a rock mass**, comprising:  
   providing a plurality of steel wires, at least one of the steel wires being formed from an Fe-based steel alloy comprising C, Si, Mn, Cr, S, and P within defined limits and balance Fe;  
   heat treating the at least one steel wire to produce a microstructure comprising retained austenite, tempered martensite, and untempered martensite; and  
   twisting the plurality of steel wires together to form a multi-strand cable bolt.

16. **The method of claim 15**, wherein the heat treating comprises a quenching step followed by a tempering step.

17. **The method of claim 15**, wherein the twisting step comprises helically twisting the plurality of steel wires to form a cable body configured for insertion into a borehole in a rock mass.

1. **A cable bolt for reinforcing a rock mass in a mining or tunneling excavation**, the cable bolt comprising:  

   a multi-strand steel cable extending along a longitudinal cable axis between a distal end and a proximal end, the multi-strand steel cable comprising a plurality of helically wound steel wires arranged to define an external cable profile having alternating ridges and grooves along the longitudinal cable axis, wherein the multi-strand steel cable has an outside diameter selected for insertion into a borehole formed in the rock mass and for receiving a settable bonding medium between the external cable profile and a wall of the borehole;  

   wherein the borehole is drilled from an exposed excavation surface into the rock mass to a predetermined embedment depth, the multi-strand steel cable being positionable in the borehole such that the distal end is located inwardly of the excavation surface and the proximal end projects outwardly from, or is accessible at, the excavation surface;  

   a fixture attached to the proximal end of the multi-strand steel cable, the fixture comprising at least one of a barrel-and-wedge anchoring assembly, a threaded termination, a swaged ferrule, a bearing plate, a nut, a washer, or a tensioning head, the fixture being configured to bear against the excavation surface directly or through a plate and to transfer tensile load from the multi-strand steel cable into the rock mass after installation;  

   wherein the multi-strand steel cable is adapted to be grouted, resin bonded, mechanically anchored, or combinations thereof within the borehole, such that load is transferred along at least a portion of the cable length by mechanical interlock, frictional engagement, chemical bonding, or a combination thereof between the cable, the bonding medium, and the rock mass; and  

   wherein each of the steel wires comprises a high-strength carbon steel having, in weight percent, carbon from 0.60 to 0.95%, manganese from 0.30 to 1.20%, silicon from 0.10 to 0.60%, phosphorus not greater than 0.030%, sulfur not greater than 0.030%, chromium not greater than 0.50%, nickel not greater than 0.50%, molybdenum not greater than 0.20%, copper not greater than 0.35%, and the balance iron and incidental impurities;  

   wherein the steel wires have a cold-drawn pearlitic microstructure comprising elongated pearlite colonies with cementite lamellae aligned generally in a drawing direction of the respective wire, and wherein the steel wires are substantially free of continuous grain-boundary cementite networks, martensitic surface layers, and decarburized surface regions having a depth greater than 2% of an individual wire diameter;  

   wherein the steel wires have a tensile strength of at least 1,500 MPa and an elongation sufficient to permit load transfer and deformation of the cable bolt under mining or tunneling ground movement without brittle fracture;  

   and wherein the external cable profile, the borehole placement, the proximal fixture, and the steel wire chemistry and microstructure are collectively selected to provide a cable bolt suitable for supporting the rock mass surrounding the mining or tunneling excavation.
